  "full_response": "Eventual consistency is a consistency model used in distributed systems, where it is not required that all nodes or replicas of a system are consistently updated and synchronized at the same time; instead, updates propagate and all replicas eventually become consistent, given enough time. The model seeks to provide availability and partition tolerance, which are desirable properties in a system experiencing network partitions, failures or live updates. Ultimately, the system converges to a known and consistent state at which no new updates can change state further.\n\n\nThis is different from strong consistency, where every operation performed on each replica must be immediately and visible to all replicas. Operationally, eventual consistency models tolerate asynchronous updates and read/write operations - processes may take some time to propagate to all nodes and should usually be limited to short durations. As such, it is not desired that users conflict with unrelated replicas while waiting for an immediate response from the system. \n\n- Availability and Partition Tolerance\n\n- Asynchronous updates\n\n- No potential for write conflicts\n\n- Consistency is achieved eventually, but different nodes may report differing data at the moment\n\n\nEventual consistency is a tradeoff which has been used extensively by data-intensive services in response to partition tolerance and availability requirements, though may not always guarantee that an up-to-date read or write operation can be performed.",
